Opt :: enum {
    Some(i32)
    None
}

// BUG: referencing a payload-less enum variant WITHOUT parentheses crashes the
// compiler (SIGSEGV), instead of compiling or producing a clean error.
//
//   x := Opt::None      // CRASHES the compiler
//   x := Opt::None()    // works fine
//
// Repro:
//   zinc tests/bugs/02_payloadless_enum_no_parens_crash.zn -o /tmp/out
// Observed:
//   Error: signal received
//   ... genType ... buildNestedFuncVar (zgen.c:2911) ... genFuncVars ...
//
// Root cause:
//   In zsem.c (~line 1704) a static-access node `Opt::None` that resolves to an
//   enum variant is retyped in place to NODE_ENUM_LIT, but the node's `call`
//   union fields (call.callee / call.args) are never initialized. The node was
//   originally a NODE_STATIC_ACCESS, so those union members hold stale/garbage
//   data. Codegen's NODE_ENUM_LIT case (zgen.c buildNestedFuncVar) then iterates
//   `node->call.args` (bogus non-zero length) and dereferences
//   `variantType->strct.fields[i]->resolved` out of bounds -> garbage pointer
//   0x2 passed to genType -> SIGSEGV.
//
//   The `Opt::None()` form goes through the NODE_CALL path which sets call.args
//   to the (empty) argument list, so it does not crash.
main :: fn () i32 {
    x := Opt::None
    return 0
}
